{"id":12,"course_id":3,"title":"Basic Networking and Resource Requests","summary":"Build the mental model for ports, DNS, CPU, memory, and scheduling.","sequence":4,"body":"## Network and capacity basics\nPods get IP addresses, Services provide stable names, and DNS lets apps call services by name. Requests describe the CPU and memory the scheduler should reserve for a Pod.\n- Container ports document what the app listens on.\n- Service ports define how other clients reach selected Pods.\n- Kubernetes DNS usually resolves `service.namespace.svc.cluster.local`.\n- CPU is compressible; memory is not. Memory pressure can kill containers.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl get svc -A\n$ kubectl exec -n payments deploy/checkout -- nslookup payments-db\n$ kubectl top pod -n payments\n$ kubectl describe node NODE_NAME\nLook for wrong ports, DNS names, missing endpoints, CPU throttling symptoms, OOMKilled, and node pressure.\n\n## Beginner tradeoff\nTiny requests can pack many Pods onto a node but produce noisy neighbors. Huge requests can strand capacity and increase cost.","practice_notes":"Scenario: Checkout cannot reach the database Service.\nChecklist:\n- Confirm the Service exists in the expected namespace.\n- Confirm it has endpoints.\n- Confirm the application uses the right port and DNS name.\n- Check NetworkPolicy only after Service and endpoints make sense.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get svc,endpointslice -n data\n$ kubectl exec -n payments deploy/checkout -- nslookup postgres.data.svc.cluster.local","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-cloud-native-foundations","course_category":"Cloud Native","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":56,"term":"Service DNS","context":"Discovery","definition":"The cluster DNS name clients use to reach a Service."},{"id":57,"term":"Container port","context":"Application port","definition":"The port an application listens on inside a container."},{"id":58,"term":"Service port","context":"Cluster port","definition":"The port exposed by a Service to clients."},{"id":59,"term":"OOMKilled","context":"Memory failure","definition":"A container termination caused by exceeding memory limits."},{"id":60,"term":"Node pressure","context":"Capacity signal","definition":"A node condition showing resource stress such as memory, disk, or PID pressure."}],"flashcards":[{"id":34,"lesson_id":12,"prompt":"What does a Service provide?","answer":"A stable virtual endpoint and DNS name for selected ready Pods.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":35,"lesson_id":12,"prompt":"Why is memory different from CPU?","answer":"CPU can throttle; memory exhaustion can kill the container.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":36,"lesson_id":12,"prompt":"What is the cost tradeoff of oversized requests?","answer":"They reserve capacity that may sit idle and increase node cost.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
